sections.s2_technical:
You create one with `Exchanger<T> ex = new Exchanger<>()`. The only essential method is `exchange(V x)`, which offers your object and blocks until the other thread also calls exchange, then returns the other thread's object. For example, the filler thread calls `empty = ex.exchange(full)` to give away its full buffer and get back an empty one, while the drainer calls `full = ex.exchange(empty)` to do the opposite.

There is a timed variant `exchange(V x, long timeout, TimeUnit unit)` that throws `TimeoutException` if no partner shows up in time, which you use so a stalled producer does not block a consumer forever.

The swap is atomic and symmetric: there is no "sender" and "receiver" role, both sides give and receive. It only ever couples two threads; if three threads call exchange, they pair up unpredictably, so Exchanger is strictly a pairwise tool, not a broadcast.

```java
Exchanger<List<Trade>> ex = new Exchanger<>();

// Filler thread
List<Trade> buffer = new ArrayList<>();
buffer.add(trade);
if (buffer.size() == CAPACITY) {
    buffer = ex.exchange(buffer);   // hand full buffer, receive empty one
    buffer.clear();
}

// Drainer thread
List<Trade> buffer = new ArrayList<>();
buffer = ex.exchange(buffer);       // hand empty buffer, receive full one
buffer.forEach(ledger::post);
```

A senior nuance: Exchanger is memory-safe. Everything the filler wrote to its buffer before `exchange` is visible to the drainer after `exchange` returns (happens-before relationship), so you do not need extra synchronization on the buffer contents. Also, because both threads reuse the same two buffer objects forever, double buffering avoids constant allocation — the two buffers just bounce back and forth. Compared with a `BlockingQueue`, Exchanger has no internal storage and couples the two threads tightly; use a queue when you want decoupling and buffering, and an Exchanger when you specifically want a paired swap with buffer reuse.

